WEBVTT

NOTE DATOM record page film. Generated by scripts/recordtour-captions.ts from recordtour/narration.json; do not hand edit.

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.790
Let's look at an example: a real DATOM record, read here from the top.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:14.050
At the top is the claim in plain words, and under it the exact claim that was checked: who, at what dose, measured how, and for how long.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.610
The record answers that question, and only that one.

4
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:24.160
51 findings from 43 studies tested it directly, and 44 say yes: 86%.

5
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:28.070
That count is where most summaries stop, and where this record starts.

6
00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:34.670
Several come from the same paper, and many share authors, labs and cohorts, so they are not 51 independent tests.

7
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:40.030
Weighted for how independent they really are, the evidence gives a range instead of a single number.

8
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:45.050
The verdict bar shows that range, from 60.6 to 74.7%.

9
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:52.330
A claim is called Supported only when the bottom of its range clears 55, and this one clears it by 5.6 points.

10
00:00:52.380 --> 00:01:01.130
So the verdict is Supported, which does not mean every study agrees, and does not mean the question is closed: even the bottom of the honest range clears the line.

11
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:07.770
The evidence grade says how solid the evidence base is, and here it is A: strong, with a narrow range.

12
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:13.210
A record is not medical advice: it checks one claim, not safety, dosing or side effects.

13
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:19.790
It says it was not commissioned or sponsored, and it dates every revision, keeping the earlier evaluation in its history.

14
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.330
The knowledge graph draws all 224 findings around the claim at the center.

15
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.270
Colour is each finding's relation to the claim, and a line is a relation a scan measured.

16
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:33.990
Drag to turn it, and select any mark to read that finding and its source.

17
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:40.230
Below it are the studies themselves, the three that weigh most first, each linked to the original paper.

18
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:46.150
A record is a dated entry, not a live feed: it changes when DATOM examines the claim again.

19
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.090
Type a claim of your own, or paste a link.

20
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.390
The box is at the bottom of the page.

21
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:53.410
DATOM.

22
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.350
Science, verified.

23
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.360
For everyone.
